<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Matches whatever corner radius the launcher uses for widget backgrounds. -->
    <dimen name="widget_corner_radius">@android:dimen/system_app_widget_background_radius</dimen>

    <!--
      How much room the rows get: padding per density band in WidgetRenderer, and the
      range a label is drawn in.

      The compact padding is what makes three rows fit a one-row cell: at 6dp a 68dp
      cell has 56dp inside it, which is the whole reason the "until" line is reachable
      at 2x1.

      The label range used to be four numbers, a name size and a footer size per band,
      and each was both a floor and a ceiling. That is what left a 2x1 and a 3x1 — the
      two sizes most people actually drop — captioned at 10sp with nothing to be done
      about it. Now WidgetRenderer measures: the floor is the smallest a label may be
      drawn at all, the ceiling is where growing one stops helping, and everything
      between is decided by the cell in front of it. 13sp is the floor because a 2x1 has
      no surplus to grow into and is still the size a label has to be legible at.

      They are dimens rather than literals because both sides need the same numbers:
      styles.xml and widget_preview.xml to declare what a raw inflation shows,
      WidgetRenderer to measure against. A number written twice is a number that ends
      up meaning two things.
    -->
    <dimen name="widget_padding">10dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_padding_compact">6dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_label_text_min">13sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_label_text_max">17sp</dimen>

    <!--
      The value's box, and the range its auto-sizing searches inside it.

      The two text sizes are read from both sides for the same reason as the metrics
      above: styles.xml hands them to `autoSizeM(in|ax)TextSize`, and WidgetRenderer
      turns them into the line heights that bound the box.

      The height is only the default the layouts declare — every variant the renderer
      builds sets its own, measured against the cell it is for. 56dp is what a 44sp
      line needs, so the default is the largest box that can ever be useful.
    -->
    <dimen name="widget_value_height">56dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_value_text_min">11sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_value_text_max">44sp</dimen>

    <dimen name="screen_margin">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="section_spacing">20dp</dimen>
</resources>
